    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "What fixes a boiler that fires but leaves the house cold?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "A boiler that fires but leaves rooms cold usually has a distribution fault. On a hot water system that means an air bound loop, a stopped circulator or a closed valve. On steam it means flooded returns and air vents that cannot breathe. Diagnosis starts on the water side and the controls, not the burner. Pressure, water level and the operating control settings decide whether heat reaches the radiators. City & Suburban staffs NATE-certified technicians, repairs all makes and models, and shows the fault before quoting a price."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "What decides the cost of boiler services, and when is the exact price known?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Boiler services pricing depends on the visit scope, including annual boiler maintenance or a diagnosed repair. Cost follows the part that failed and the work needed to reach it. A radiator vent or a thermocouple on an accessible steam boiler sits at one end of the range. A leaking section, a seized circulator, a failed gas valve or a control board on a wall hung boiler sits at the other. Age matters, because a boiler built decades ago may need a special order control. Access matters too: a crawlspace or a locked shared basement adds time. City & Suburban prices upfront with no hidden fees and puts the exact figure in writing before work starts."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "What happens during a boiler services visit?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "A boiler repair visit starts with the complaint, the thermostat and the boiler controls. Gas pressure and ignition are checked next. On a steam boiler the low water cutoff is blown down and the gauge glass is read. On a hot water system the relief valve, expansion tank and circulator are inspected. Water level, pressure and flue condition are recorded. A repair that uses a part carried on the van is usually finished the same visit, while a special order control means a return trip. City & Suburban explains the fault and the options at the boiler, and the price is written down before work is authorized."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Which boiler symptoms should not wait until morning?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Four symptoms qualify. A gas smell. Water pouring from the boiler or a relief valve discharging. A carbon monoxide alarm sounding. No heat at all in freezing weather. A gas odor means leaving the building and calling the utility first. The others mean switching the boiler off at its service switch, then closing the water feed if a leak is spreading. City & Suburban offers emergency HVAC service every day till midnight, except Sundays, and same day service when the schedule allows. Knocking pipes, a short cycling burner or one cold radiator can wait for a scheduled heater repair visit."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "How can annual boiler maintenance help address radiator heat problems?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Annual boiler maintenance is a chance to discuss radiator heat before winter. On a hot water loop a single cold radiator is almost always trapped air or a partly closed valve. Bleeding the radiator clears trapped air. On a steam system the usual causes are a dead air vent on that radiator or a failed thermostatic valve. A pitch problem that lets condensate pool in the pipe does the same. A whole floor going cold points somewhere else: the boiler, the circulator, or steam pressure set too high for the pipe runs. City & Suburban shows the fault before quoting and puts the exact price in writing before work starts. City & Suburban maintenance plans include seasonal check-ups, priority dispatch and member discounts."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Are boiler services available for a shared boiler in a two-flat or greystone?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Yes. Chicago two-flats and greystones often run one boiler for both units, with radiators split across floors and the only thermostat in the lower unit. City & Suburban covers 21 service areas, 13 Chicago neighborhoods and 8 suburbs. Repairs cover all makes and models, which matters where the boiler is older than every resident. Cook County Assessor records of residential property characteristics in the eight Chicago townships show a median year built of 1927. Visits to a shared building are scheduled around access to both units. Service areas has the full map."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "What does the Chicago Heat Ordinance require of a building heated by a boiler?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "From September 15 to June 1 the general rule in the Chicago Municipal Code is at least 68 degrees from 8:30 a.m. to 10:30 p.m. The same rule sets 66 degrees from 10:30 p.m. to 8:30 a.m. where the landlord supplies the heat. The code also lists exceptions (Chicago Municipal Code 13-196-410). In a two-flat with one boiler that duty belongs to the owner, not the tenant, so a stopped circulator becomes a compliance problem overnight. City & Suburban offers same day service when the schedule allows, plus emergency HVAC service every day till midnight, except Sundays."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Does the age of a boiler put a repair out of reach?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Rarely, and not because of age alone. Cast iron sectional boilers from the middle of the last century still run in Chicago. The heat exchanger is heavy and the controls are simple. What ends a boiler is a cracked or leaking section, a flooded burner tray, or a flue that cannot be made safe. Controls, vents, circulators and gas valves are still sold or have modern equivalents. City & Suburban repairs all makes and models and replaces the failed component where that is the honest answer. William G. wrote in March 2024: “Rob Koehler showed up on time and fixed my boiler for a reasonable charge. I now had heat. Victory!”"
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "When should a boiler visit include a discussion of other heating options?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "A boiler suits the building it was designed for: radiators, solid masonry walls, and rooms that hold steady heat without blowing dust around. It is the wrong answer for a home that needs central cooling as well as heat. It is also the wrong answer for a converted attic or rear addition that was never piped for heat. Those spaces are usually better served by a heat pump. City & Suburban services boilers and heat pumps, so the recommendation is not narrowed to one format."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "What evidence supports City & Suburban boiler services?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Boiler customers name the work. Marianne Y. wrote in September 2025: “Rob has been servicing my boiler for several years. He very knowledgeable, professional and a nice guy. Definitely would recommend this company.” William G. wrote in March 2024: “When I had a problem with my boiler I called the first name I saw in the book.” Beyond the boiler reviews, City & Suburban holds a 4.98 average across 204 Google reviews and 4.9 from 251 reviews on Yelp. The company also holds an A+ rating from the BBB and staffs NATE-certified technicians."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "What safety devices belong in a house heated by a boiler?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "A working carbon monoxide alarm comes first. Illinois requires alarms near sleeping areas in dwellings under the Carbon Monoxide Alarm Detector Act (430 ILCS 135). On the boiler the safety chain is the pressure relief valve, the low water cutoff on a steam system, and the flue that carries combustion gases outside. A relief valve that weeps or a cutoff that has never been blown down is a reason to shut the boiler off and call. Rust flakes under the flue hood are another. A boiler services visit tests those controls. City & Suburban repairs all makes and models and shows what failed before quoting a price."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "What does annual boiler maintenance include that a furnace tune-up does not?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Water. Annual boiler maintenance blows down the low water cutoff, checks the gauge glass and tests the relief valve. A steam boiler making wet steam is skimmed, and operating pressure is set low, where a steam system is designed to run. A hot water system instead gets the expansion tank charge, the circulator and the air separator checked. None of that exists on a furnace, which is why heater maintenance is a separate visit with a separate checklist. City & Suburban builds seasonal check ups, priority dispatch and member discounts into its maintenance plans."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Does a Chicago boiler repair or replacement need a City permit?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Component repairs generally do not. Replacing the equipment does, because installed heating equipment is permitted work in Chicago. City of Chicago building permit records for the 24 months to August 2026 mention 108 boiler permits, a small share next to furnace work. A permit adds inspection time to the schedule, and the cost sits inside the written price rather than beside it. Boiler replacements in Chicago generally need a City permit, and the written quote should say who pulls it. City & Suburban repairs all makes and models and prices upfront with no hidden fees."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "What should a Chicago homeowner ask before booking steam boiler services?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Five questions sort the field. Ask how many steam systems a contractor works on in a Chicago winter, because one-pipe steam behaves nothing like a hot water loop. Ask whether the person who diagnoses the fault is the person who sells the replacement. Ask for the exact price in writing before work starts. Ask whether repairs cover all makes and models, which decides who can touch a cast iron boiler. Ask for license and insurance details. City & Suburban has been family owned since 1952, is run by Rob of the second generation, and prices upfront with no hidden fees."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "What keeps a boiler and radiator system out of trouble between seasons?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Four habits. Book annual service before September 15, when the Chicago Heat Ordinance season starts and every contractor takes the first cold calls at once. Keep furniture, covers and curtains clear of radiators so heat reaches the room. Report a new noise, a weeping valve or a cold radiator while the weather is still mild and the schedule is open. Keep a working carbon monoxide alarm on every sleeping floor, as the Illinois Carbon Monoxide Alarm Detector Act requires near sleeping areas. City & Suburban builds seasonal check ups and priority dispatch into its maintenance plans."
     }
    },
